Connected Components Tutorial -- clarifying the difference between graph and hypergraph components
**************************************************************************************************

In this tutorial, we discuss the various methods that deal with connected
components of graphs and hypergraphs. Our main objective is to make a
distinction between the two different definitions of connected components that
are used in the *note EdgeIdeals: Top, package.

A vertex of a (hyper)graph H said to be an isolated vertex if it is not
contained in any edge of H. In particular, if a vertex of H is contained in a
edge of size one then it is not considered isolated.



+---------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[u,v,x,y,z];          |
+---------------------------------+
|i2 : H = hyperGraph({{u,v},{x}});|
+---------------------------------+
|i3 : isolatedVertices H          |
|                                 |
|o3 = {y, z}                      |
|                                 |
|o3 : List                        |
+---------------------------------+

Graph Components. A connected component of a graph is any maximal set of
vertices which are pairwise connected by a (possibly trivial) path. The most
important part of this definition is that isolated vertices count as connected
components.

The following methods use this definition of a connected component: *note
connectedGraphComponents: connectedGraphComponents,, *note
numConnectedGraphComponents: numConnectedGraphComponents, and *note
isConnectedGraph: isConnectedGraph,.

Hypergraph Components. A connected component of a hypergraph is any maximal set
of vertices which are pairwise connected by a non-trivial path. Here isolated
vertices do not count as connected components.

The following methods use the hypergraph definition of a connected component:
*note connectedComponents:
(SimplicialComplexes)connectedComponents_lpSimplicialComplex_rp,, *note
numConnectedComponents: numConnectedComponents, and *note isConnected:
isConnected,.

The next example uses all of these methods on a graph to illustrate the
difference between the two definitions.



+----------------------------------+
|i4 : R = QQ[u,v,x,y,z];           |
+----------------------------------+
|i5 : G = graph({{x,y},{y,z}});    |
+----------------------------------+
|i6 : isolatedVertices G           |
|                                  |
|o6 = {u, v}                       |
|                                  |
|o6 : List                         |
+----------------------------------+
|i7 : connectedGraphComponents G   |
|                                  |
|o7 = {{u}, {v}, {x, y, z}}        |
|                                  |
|o7 : List                         |
+----------------------------------+
|i8 : numConnectedGraphComponents G|
|                                  |
|o8 = 3                            |
+----------------------------------+
|i9 : isConnectedGraph G           |
|                                  |
|o9 = false                        |
+----------------------------------+
|i10 : connectedComponents G       |
|                                  |
|o10 = {{x, y, z}}                 |
|                                  |
|o10 : List                        |
+----------------------------------+
|i11 : numConnectedComponents G    |
|                                  |
|o11 = 1                           |
+----------------------------------+
|i12 : isConnected G               |
|                                  |
|o12 = true                        |
+----------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note connectedComponents:
    (SimplicialComplexes)connectedComponents_lpSimplicialComplex_rp, -- find
    the connected components of an abstract simplicial complex
  * *note connectedGraphComponents: connectedGraphComponents, -- returns the
    connected components of a graph
  * *note isConnected: isConnected, -- determines if a (hyper)graph is
    connected
  * *note isConnectedGraph: isConnectedGraph, -- determines if a graph is
    connected
  * *note isolatedVertices: isolatedVertices, -- returns all vertices not
    contained in any edge
  * *note numConnectedComponents: numConnectedComponents, -- returns the number
    of connected components in a (hyper)graph
  * *note numConnectedGraphComponents: numConnectedGraphComponents, -- returns
    the number of connected components in a graph
