randomHyperGraph -- returns a random hypergraph
***********************************************

  * Usage: 
        H = randomHyperGraph(R,D)
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note polynomial ring: (Macaulay2Doc)PolynomialRing,, which gives
        the vertex set of H
      * D, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, of integers that are the
        cardinalities of the edges of H
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note BranchLimit: randomHyperGraph_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmBranchLimit=>_pd_pd_
        pd_rp, => ..., default value 3, limit recursive branching in
        randomHyperGraph
      * *note TimeLimit: randomHyperGraph_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmTimeLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp
        , => ..., default value 5, limit the running time of randomHyperGraph
  * Outputs:
      * H, a *note hypergraph: HyperGraph,, a hypergraph with edges E_i each of
        size D_i. Returns null if none can be found.

Description
===========

This method is not guaranteed to return a HyperGraph, even if one exists with
the given edge sizes. This method searches for a hypergraph with the given edge
sizes using a random recursive algorithm. Limits can be placed on the both
number of recursive steps taken (see *note BranchLimit: BranchLimit,) and on
the time taken (see *note TimeLimit: TimeLimit,). The method will return null
if it cannot find a hypergraph within the branch and time limits.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x_1..x_5];                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : randomHyperGraph(R,{3,2,4})                                             |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : randomHyperGraph(R,{3,2,4})                                             |
|                                                                             |
|o3 = HyperGraph{"edges" => {{x , x , x }, {x , x }, {x , x , x , x }}}       |
|                              3   4   5     2   3     1   2   4   5          |
|                "ring" => R                                                  |
|                "vertices" => {x , x , x , x , x }                           |
|                                1   2   3   4   5                            |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : HyperGraph                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : randomHyperGraph(R,{3,2,4})                                             |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = HyperGraph{"edges" => {{x , x , x }, {x , x }, {x , x , x , x }}}       |
|                              1   3   4     3   5     1   2   4   5          |
|                "ring" => R                                                  |
|                "vertices" => {x , x , x , x , x }                           |
|                                1   2   3   4   5                            |
|                                                                             |
|o4 : HyperGraph                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : randomHyperGraph(R,{4,4,2,2}) -- impossible, returns null when          |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|time/branch limit reached                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The randomHyperGraph method will return null immediately if the sizes of the
edges fail to pass the LYM-inequality: $1/(n choose D_1) + 1/(n choose D_2) +
... + 1/(n choose D_m) \leq 1$ where $n$ is the number of variables in R and
$m$ is the length of D. Note that even if D passes this inequality, it is not
necessarily true that there is some hypergraph with edge sizes given by D. See
D. Lubell's "A short proof of Sperner's lemma," J. Combin. Theory, 1:299
(1966).

See also
========

  * *note randomGraph: randomGraph, -- returns a random graph
  * *note randomUniformHyperGraph: randomUniformHyperGraph, -- returns a random
    uniform hypergraph
  * *note BranchLimit: BranchLimit, -- optional argument for randomHyperGraph
  * *note TimeLimit: TimeLimit, -- optional argument for randomHyperGraph
  * *note Constructor Overview: Constructor Overview, -- a summary of the many
    ways of making graphs and hypergraphs

Ways to use randomHyperGraph:
=============================

  * "randomHyperGraph(PolynomialRing,List)"

